  "findings": [
    "The cost at 100K was not sorting but deciding: sessions decided the predicate on every retained record (about 1 us each, memory-bound) before sorting the matches, so sorted top-50 and plain path-order pages scaled linearly.",
    "Walking the records in the frontend's order and stopping after offset + limit matches makes the work proportional to the page: at 100K one-reader p95 fell from 49-98 ms to 3.0-4.9 ms per frontend, and under eight readers with two writes per second p50/p95/p99 fell from 115/152/179 ms to 5.8/8.7/20.7 ms.",
    "Sort keys correlate with predicates in practice (titles grouped by type put every task last), so the walk reads a cached match bitset rather than deciding each record it passes.",
    "Indexes must survive writes: rebuilding them per snapshot regressed tails under writes on the development host, and carrying only the immediate predecessor's indexes still rebuilt most of them at 100K. Generational carry with single-flight computation removes both."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "A change of identities (create, delete, rename, alias change) rebuilds each index on first use: about 65-115 ms per order or match set at 100K on the development host, once per query shape.",
    "Only plain sort keys walk: notes sort keys, Bases plain properties or row values, and DQL single identifier keys whose values are nulls plus one plain kind. Other queries decide every candidate as before.",
    "Direct (daemon-less) CLI queries hold no records up front and are unchanged."
  ]
}
